Subtask 1.3: Configure Istio Traffic Routing Rules
Create gateway.yaml:
apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: Gateway
metadata:
  name: product-gateway
  namespace: canary-demo
spec:
  selector:
    istio: ingressgateway
  servers:
  - port:
      number: 80
      name: http
      protocol: HTTP
    hosts:
    - "*"
Create destination-rule.yaml:
apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: DestinationRule
metadata:
  name: product-service-destination
  namespace: canary-demo
spec:
  host: product-service
  subsets:
  - name: stable
    labels:
      version: stable
  - name: canary
    labels:
      version: canary
Create virtual-service.yaml with a 90/10 traffic split:
apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: VirtualService
metadata:
  name: product-service-vs
  namespace: canary-demo
spec:
  hosts:
  - "*"
  gateways:
  - product-gateway
  http:
  - match:
    - uri:
        prefix: "/"
    route:
    - destination:
        host: product-service
        subset: stable
      weight: 90
    - destination:
        host: product-service
        subset: canary
      weight: 10
Apply and verify all Istio configurations:
kubectl apply -f gateway.yaml
kubectl apply -f destination-rule.yaml
kubectl apply -f virtual-service.yaml

kubectl get gateway -n canary-demo
kubectl get destinationrule -n canary-demo
kubectl get virtualservice -n canary-demo
Deliverable: All three resources (product-gateway, product-service-destination, product-service-vs) are listed with no errors.

Subtask 1.4: Test Traffic Distribution
Get the gateway address. On cloud providers with a LoadBalancer this returns an external IP; in local/kind clusters use the NodePort instead:
kubectl get svc istio-ingressgateway -n istio-system

export GATEWAY_URL=$(kubectl get svc istio-ingressgateway -n istio-system \
  -o jsonpath='{.status.loadBalancer.ingress[0].ip}')

if [ -z "$GATEWAY_URL" ]; then
  export INGRESS_PORT=$(kubectl get svc istio-ingressgateway -n istio-system \
    -o jsonpath='{.spec.ports[?(@.name=="http2")].nodePort}')
  export NODE_IP=$(kubectl get nodes -o jsonpath='{.items[0].status.addresses[?(@.type=="InternalIP")].address}')
  export GATEWAY_URL="$NODE_IP:$INGRESS_PORT"
fi

echo "Gateway URL: $GATEWAY_URL"
Create test-traffic.sh:
#!/bin/bash
stable_count=0
canary_count=0
total_requests=50

echo "Testing traffic distribution with $total_requests requests..."
echo "Expected: approximately 90% stable, 10% canary"
echo "----------------------------------------"

for i in $(seq 1 $total_requests); do
    response=$(curl -s "http://$GATEWAY_URL/" | grep -o "Version: [A-Za-z]*")
    if [[ $response == *"Stable"* ]]; then
        stable_count=$((stable_count + 1))
    elif [[ $response == *"Canary"* ]]; then
        canary_count=$((canary_count + 1))
    fi
done

stable_percentage=$((stable_count * 100 / total_requests))
canary_percentage=$((canary_count * 100 / total_requests))

echo "----------------------------------------"
echo "Results:"
echo "Stable version: $stable_count requests (${stable_percentage}%)"
echo "Canary version: $canary_count requests (${canary_percentage}%)"
echo "Total requests: $total_requests"
Run the test:
chmod +x test-traffic.sh
./test-traffic.sh
Deliverable: Output shows both versions receiving traffic, roughly in the configured 90/10 proportion (exact percentages will vary due to randomness, but canary should be noticeably lower than stable).

Task 2: Monitor Health of Stable and Canary Versions
Subtask 2.1: Create a Canary Monitoring Script
Create monitor-canary.sh:
#!/bin/bash

NAMESPACE="canary-demo"

echo "=== Canary Deployment Monitoring ==="
echo "Timestamp: $(date)"
echo "======================================"

echo "1. Pod Health Status:"
echo "Stable pods:"
kubectl get pods -n $NAMESPACE -l version=stable

echo -e "\nCanary pods:"
kubectl get pods -n $NAMESPACE -l version=canary

echo -e "\n2. Resource Usage (requires metrics-server):"
kubectl top pods -n $NAMESPACE -l version=stable 2>/dev/null || echo "Metrics not available for stable"
kubectl top pods -n $NAMESPACE -l version=canary 2>/dev/null || echo "Metrics not available for canary"

echo -e "\n3. Response Time Check:"
response_time=$(curl -o /dev/null -s -w "%{time_total}" "http://$GATEWAY_URL/" || echo "0")
echo "Sample response time: ${response_time}s"

echo -e "\n4. Error Rate Check (10 requests):"
error_count=0
total_requests=10

for i in $(seq 1 $total_requests); do
    http_code=$(curl -o /dev/null -s -w "%{http_code}" "http://$GATEWAY_URL/")
    if [ "$http_code" != "200" ]; then
        error_count=$((error_count + 1))
    fi
done

error_rate=$((error_count * 100 / total_requests))
echo "Error rate: ${error_rate}% (${error_count}/${total_requests} requests failed)"

echo -e "\n5. Current Traffic Distribution (20 requests):"
stable_count=0
canary_count=0
test_requests=20

for i in $(seq 1 $test_requests); do
    response=$(curl -s "http://$GATEWAY_URL/" | grep -o "Version: [A-Za-z]*")
    if [[ $response == *"Stable"* ]]; then
        stable_count=$((stable_count + 1))
    elif [[ $response == *"Canary"* ]]; then
        canary_count=$((canary_count + 1))
    fi
done

stable_pct=$((stable_count * 100 / test_requests))
canary_pct=$((canary_count * 100 / test_requests))

echo "Stable: ${stable_pct}% ($stable_count/$test_requests)"
echo "Canary: ${canary_pct}% ($canary_count/$test_requests)"
echo "======================================"
Run the monitoring script:
chmod +x monitor-canary.sh
./monitor-canary.sh
Deliverable: The script prints pod status for both versions, an error rate of 0% under normal conditions, and a traffic split consistent with the VirtualService weights.

Subtask 2.2: Gradually Increase Canary Traffic
Create gradual-rollout.sh, which shifts more traffic to canary in stages while checking health between each stage:
#!/bin/bash

NAMESPACE="canary-demo"
VIRTUAL_SERVICE="product-service-vs"

STAGES=(10 25 50)
WAIT_TIME=20

echo "Starting gradual canary rollout..."
echo "Stages: ${STAGES[@]}%"

for stage in "${STAGES[@]}"; do
    stable_weight=$((100 - stage))
    canary_weight=$stage

    echo "=== Stage: ${canary_weight}% canary traffic ==="

    kubectl patch virtualservice $VIRTUAL_SERVICE -n $NAMESPACE --type='merge' -p="{
        \"spec\": {
            \"http\": [{
                \"match\": [{\"uri\": {\"prefix\": \"/\"}}],
                \"route\": [
                    {\"destination\": {\"host\": \"product-service\", \"subset\": \"stable\"}, \"weight\": $stable_weight},
                    {\"destination\": {\"host\": \"product-service\", \"subset\": \"canary\"}, \"weight\": $canary_weight}
                ]
            }]
        }
    }"

    echo "Updated traffic split: ${stable_weight}% stable, ${canary_weight}% canary"
    echo "Waiting ${WAIT_TIME}s for traffic to stabilize..."
    sleep $WAIT_TIME

    echo "Running health check..."
    ./monitor-canary.sh | grep -E "(Error rate|Stable:|Canary:)"

    echo "Stage ${canary_weight}% completed"
    echo "----------------------------------------"
done

echo "Gradual rollout completed. Current state: 50% stable, 50% canary"
Run the gradual rollout:
chmod +x gradual-rollout.sh
./gradual-rollout.sh
Deliverable: The VirtualService weight progresses through 10 percent, 25 percent, and 50 percent canary, confirmed by kubectl get virtualservice product-service-vs -n canary-demo -o yaml showing weight: 50 for the canary subset at the end, with a 0 percent error rate reported at each stage.

Task 3: Detect a Problem and Roll Back the Canary
Subtask 3.1: Create Rollback Scripts
Create emergency-rollback.sh to immediately route all traffic to stable:
#!/bin/bash

NAMESPACE="canary-demo"
VIRTUAL_SERVICE="product-service-vs"

echo "=== EMERGENCY ROLLBACK INITIATED ==="
echo "Timestamp: $(date)"
echo "Routing 100% traffic to stable version..."

kubectl patch virtualservice $VIRTUAL_SERVICE -n $NAMESPACE --type='merge' -p='{
    "spec": {
        "http": [{
            "match": [{"uri": {"prefix": "/"}}],
            "route": [
                {"destination": {"host": "product-service", "subset": "stable"}, "weight": 100}
            ]
        }]
    }
}'

echo "Traffic routing updated. Verifying rollback..."
sleep 5

stable_count=0
canary_count=0
test_requests=10

for i in $(seq 1 $test_requests); do
    response=$(curl -s "http://$GATEWAY_URL/" | grep -o "Version: [A-Za-z]*")
    if [[ $response == *"Stable"* ]]; then
        stable_count=$((stable_count + 1))
    elif [[ $response == *"Canary"* ]]; then
        canary_count=$((canary_count + 1))
    fi
done

echo "Rollback verification: Stable=$stable_count/$test_requests Canary=$canary_count/$test_requests"

if [ $stable_count -eq $test_requests ]; then
    echo "ROLLBACK SUCCESSFUL - All traffic routed to stable version"
else
    echo "ROLLBACK VERIFICATION FAILED - Manual intervention required"
fi

echo "=== ROLLBACK COMPLETED ==="
Make it executable:
chmod +x emergency-rollback.sh
Subtask 3.2: Deploy a Problematic Canary and Route Traffic to It
Create problematic-canary.yaml. This version uses a deliberately broken configuration (a nonexistent volume mount path override causing nginx to serve a 404) to simulate a canary defect:
apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: problematic-canary-content
  namespace: canary-demo
data:
  broken.html: |
    <!DOCTYPE html>
    <html>
    <head><title>Product Service - Problematic Version</title></head>
    <body>
        <h1>Product Service</h1>
        <h2>Version: Problematic v2.1</h2>
        <div style="background-color: red; color: white; padding: 10px;">
            Status: ERROR - This version is problematic
        </div>
    </body>
    </html>
---
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: product-service-problematic
  namespace: canary-demo
  labels:
    app: product-service
    version: problematic
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: product-service
      version: problematic
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: product-service
        version: problematic
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: product-service
        image: nginx:1.21
        ports:
        - containerPort: 80
        env:
        - name: VERSION
          value: "problematic-v2.1"
        volumeMounts:
        - name: html-content
          mountPath: /usr/share/nginx/html
        # index.html is intentionally not provided by this ConfigMap,
        # so nginx returns HTTP 404 on the default path, simulating a
        # broken canary release.
      volumes:
      - name: html-content
        configMap:
          name: problematic-canary-content
Deploy the problematic version:
kubectl apply -f problematic-canary.yaml
kubectl get pods -n canary-demo -l version=problematic
Update the DestinationRule to add the problematic subset:
