Expected Outcomes
A working canary deployment with Istio traffic splitting between stable and canary subsets, verified by observed request percentages matching configured weights
A repeatable monitoring workflow that measures error rate and traffic distribution on demand
A demonstrated rollback: a deliberately broken problematic subset produced measurable errors, and the emergency rollback script restored 100 percent traffic to stable with a 0 percent error rate
Troubleshooting
Issue 1: All traffic goes to one version regardless of configured weights. Symptom: test-traffic.sh or monitor-canary.sh reports 100 percent to a single version even though the VirtualService specifies a split. Diagnostic hint: check whether the namespace has istio-injection=enabled and whether existing pods were created before the label was applied (kubectl get pods -n canary-demo -o jsonpath='{.items[*].spec.containers[*].name}' to confirm an istio-proxy sidecar is present); pods created before injection was enabled will not have a sidecar and must be restarted with kubectl rollout restart deployment/<name> -n canary-demo.

Issue 2: curl requests to $GATEWAY_URL time out or are refused. Symptom: Traffic test scripts hang or return empty responses. Diagnostic hint: re-check how the gateway address was derived in Subtask 1.4 â�� clusters without a cloud LoadBalancer will not populate .status.loadBalancer.ingress[0].ip, so confirm whether you should be using the NodePort and node IP fallback instead, and verify the Gateway and VirtualService both reference the same host and gateway name (kubectl describe virtualservice product-service-vs -n canary-demo).

Conclusion
In this lab, you implemented a complete canary release workflow on Kubernetes using Istio: deploying stable and canary versions of a microservice, splitting traffic between them with a Gateway, DestinationRule, and VirtualService, and progressively increasing canary exposure while monitoring error rates and response times. You then simulated a real failure scenario by deploying a deliberately broken canary subset, routing live traffic to it, observing the resulting error rate increase, and executing both emergency and gradual rollback procedures to restore full traffic to the stable version. The key takeaway is that canary releases are only safe when paired with active health monitoring and a tested, fast rollback path â�� traffic-splitting configuration alone is not sufficient without the ability to detect problems and revert quickly. These same patterns of gradual exposure, continuous health checks, and automated or scripted rollback form the foundation of safe progressive delivery practices used in production Kubernetes environments.
